https://www.redhat.com/en/topics/devops/what-cicd-pipeline

[[1. Prerequisites]]

* Git fundamentals: branches, merges, rebasing, tags, pull/merge requests
* Command line basics: Bash, file permissions, environment variables, exit codes
* YAML syntax: pipeline configs are almost always YAML (indentation, lists, maps, anchors)
* Basic Linux: package managers, processes, SSH

[[2. Core Concepts]]

* CI (Continuous Integration): automatically build and test on every push
* CD: Delivery (always deployable, manual release) vs Deployment (auto-released to production)
* Pipeline anatomy: stages, jobs, steps, triggers
* Runners/agents: the machines that execute jobs (shared vs self-hosted)
* Artifacts vs cache: artifacts pass build outputs between jobs; cache speeds up dependency installs
* Pipeline triggers: push, merge request, schedule, tag, manual, API

[[3. Pipeline Stages (the typical flow)]]

* Build: compile code, bundle assets
* Test: unit, integration, end-to-end
* Lint/static analysis: code style, type checks
* Security scanning: SAST, dependency scanning, secret detection
* Package: build Docker images, publish to a registry
* Deploy: staging, then production

[[4. Tooling]]

Pick one platform to learn deeply first:

* GitLab CI/CD (.gitlab-ci.yml): fits your GitLab setup
* GitHub Actions (.github/workflows/*.yml)
* Jenkins (Jenkinsfile): common in older enterprises

Supporting tools:

* Docker: building images, Dockerfiles, multi-stage builds, registries
* Test frameworks for your language (pytest, Jest, JUnit, etc.)
* Package registries: npm, PyPI, container registry

[[5. Intermediate Topics]]

* Secrets management (CI variables, masked/protected variables, Vault)
* Environment-specific config (dev/staging/prod)
* Conditional jobs (rules, only/except, needs)
* Parallel and matrix jobs
* Reusable templates and includes
* Pipeline optimization: caching, DAG pipelines, fail-fast
* Branching strategies: trunk-based, GitFlow, feature branches

[[6. Deployment Strategies]]

* Rolling updates
* Blue/green deployments
* Canary releases
* Feature flags
* Rollback procedures

[[7. Infrastructure & Advanced]]

* Infrastructure as Code: Terraform, Ansible
* Kubernetes and Helm basics
* GitOps: Argo CD, Flux
* Observability: logs, metrics, alerting post-deploy
* Pipeline security: least-privilege tokens, supply chain security, signed artifacts

Suggested Order

# Git + YAML + Bash
# Write a simple pipeline: lint, then test, on every push
# Add Docker build and push to a registry
# Add a deploy job to a staging environment
# Add secrets, caching, and rules
# Explore Terraform, Kubernetes, and GitOps

Practice Project Idea

Take a small web app and build a pipeline that lints, tests, builds a Docker image, scans it, deploys to staging automatically, and deploys to production on manual approval.